ASIO hands doubles to a callback that wants them, and converts its 64-bit float formats

ASIOSampleFormat converts a driver's samples to floats and back: the integer formats through a double that is rounded to a float at the end, the 32-bit float formats by copying their bytes. So a callback that computes in double precision has its samples rounded to a float before they are rounded to the driver's width. The 64-bit float formats, ASIOSTFloat64LSB and ASIOSTFloat64MSB, fall to the jassertfalse of the default case in both directions, which leaves the callback's input buffers holding what they held and the driver's output buffers unwritten.

The conversions are templates on the sample type -- convertToSamples() and convertFromSamples() in place of convertToFloat() and convertFromFloat() -- so an integer sample goes straight to and from a double, a 32-bit float is widened or rounded in the driver's byte order, and the 64-bit float formats are converted, in either precision and either byte order. The device keeps buffers of doubles beside its buffers of floats, allocated and pointed into with them, and calls the callback's double method while the callback wants doubles, asked under the callback lock before each block. With floats every conversion is the one it was, except that the 64-bit float formats are converted now.

It builds on the patch that adds the callback's double method.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is upstream.

